#: ../../../blog/2019/macos-memory-maps.rst:9
msgid "Removing Process.memory_maps() on macOS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2019/macos-memory-maps.rst:11
msgid ""
"This is part of the psutil 5.6.0 release (see the full :doc:`release notes </"
"blog/2019/announcing-560>`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2019/macos-memory-maps.rst:14
msgid "As of 5.6.0, :meth:`Process.memory_maps` is no longer defined on macOS."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2019/macos-memory-maps.rst:17
msgid "The bug"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2019/macos-memory-maps.rst:19
msgid ""
":gh:`1291`: on macOS, :meth:`Process.memory_maps` would either raise "
"``OSError: [Errno 22] Invalid argument`` or segfault the whole Python "
"process! Both triggered from code as simple as ``psutil.Process()."
"as_dict()``, since :meth:`~Process.as_dict` iterates every attribute, and :"
"meth:`~Process.memory_maps` is one of them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2019/macos-memory-maps.rst:25
msgid ""
"The root cause was inside Apple's undocumented ``proc_regionfilename()`` "
"syscall. On some memory regions it returns ``EINVAL``. On others it takes "
"the process down. Which regions? Nobody figured out. Arnon Yaari (:user:"
"`wiggin15`) did most of the investigation: he wrote a `standalone C "
"reproducer <https://gist.github.com/"
"wiggin15/0a4c51b5bc6c52e6e31e2234f88558ab>`__ and walked me through what "
"he'd tried."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2019/macos-memory-maps.rst:33
msgid ""
"In :pr:`1436` I attempted a fix by reverse-engineering ``vmmap(1)`` but it "
"didn't work. The fundamental problem is that ``vmmap`` is closed source and "
"``proc_regionfilename`` is undocumented. Neither my virtualized macOS "
"(10.11.6) nor Travis CI (10.12.1) could reproduce the bug, which reproduced "
"reliably only on 10.14.3."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2019/macos-memory-maps.rst:40
msgid "Why remove outright"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2019/macos-memory-maps.rst:42
msgid ""
"While removing the C code I noticed that the macOS unit test had been "
"disabled long ago, presumably by me after recurring flaky Travis runs. "
"Meaning that the method had been broken on some macOS versions far longer "
"than the 2018 bug report suggested."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2019/macos-memory-maps.rst:47
msgid ""
"Deprecating for a cycle didn't help either: raising :exc:`AccessDenied` "
"breaks code that relied on a successful return, returning an empty list does "
"the same silently, and leaving the method in place doesn't stop the "
"segfault. Basically there was no sane solution, so since 5.6 is a major "
"version I decided to just remove :meth:`Process.memory_maps` for good."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2019/macos-memory-maps.rst:53
msgid ""
"On macOS it never supported other processes anyway. Calling it on any PID "
"other than the current one (or its children) raised :exc:`AccessDenied`, "
"even as root."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2019/macos-memory-maps.rst:57
msgid ""
"If someone finds a Mach API path that works, the method can return. Nobody "
"has found one so far."
msgstr ""
